    "# Crowd Counting\n",
    "\n",
    "## ShanghaiTech\n",
    "\n",
    "The [ShanghaiTech Crowd Counting Dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/tthien/shanghaitech) is one of the most widely used benchmarks for **crowd counting**, **density estimation**, and **crowd localization** in computer vision. It was introduced in the influential paper [Single-Image Crowd Counting via Multi-Column Convolutional Neural Network (MCNN)](https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/7780439/) and has become a standard dataset for evaluating crowd-counting algorithms.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Property               | Value                                                  |\n",
    "| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |\n",
    "| Year Released          | 2016                                                   |\n",
    "| Total Images           | 1,198                                                  |\n",
    "| Total Annotated People | 330,165                                                |\n",
    "| Annotation Type        | Head-center point annotations                          |\n",
    "| Main Tasks             | Crowd Counting, Density Estimation, Crowd Localization |\n",
    "| Subsets                | Part A and Part B                                      |\n",
    "\n",
    "Each person in an image is labeled with a **single dot near the center of the head**. These point annotations are typically converted into density maps using Gaussian kernels during training. \n",
    "\n",
    "### Dataset Structure\n",
    "\n",
    "The dataset is divided into two very different subsets.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Part A (ShanghaiTech-A)\n",
    "\n",
    "| Property            | Value    |\n",
    "| ------------------- | -------- |\n",
    "| Images              | 482      |\n",
    "| Train               | 300      |\n",
    "| Test                | 182      |\n",
    "| Total People        | ~241,677 |\n",
    "| Average Count/Image | ~501     |\n",
    "| Maximum Count       | 3,139    |\n",
    "| Minimum Count       | 33       |\n",
    "\n",
    "Characteristics:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Collected from the Internet.\n",
    "* Extremely dense crowd scenes.\n",
    "* Large scale variation.\n",
    "* Severe occlusion.\n",
    "* Strong perspective distortion.\n",
    "* Designed to challenge high-density crowd counting methods.\n",
    "\n",
    "Typical examples include:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Religious gatherings\n",
    "* Concerts\n",
    "* Public events\n",
    "* Demonstrations\n",
    "* Stadium crowds\n",
    "\n",
    "Part A is generally considered much harder than Part B because individual heads may occupy only a few pixels.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Part B (ShanghaiTech-B)\n",
    "\n",
    "| Property            | Value             |\n",
    "| ------------------- | ----------------- |\n",
    "| Images              | 716               |\n",
    "| Train               | 400               |\n",
    "| Test                | 316               |\n",
    "| Total People        | ~88,488           |\n",
    "| Average Count/Image | ~124              |\n",
    "| Maximum Count       | 578               |\n",
    "| Minimum Count       | 9                 |\n",
    "| Resolution          | Mostly 768 × 1024 |\n",
    "\n",
    "Characteristics:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Collected from busy streets in Shanghai.\n",
    "* Lower crowd density.\n",
    "* More regular surveillance-style viewpoints.\n",
    "* Less severe occlusion.\n",
    "* More suitable for evaluating generalization in urban scenes.\n",
    "\n",
    "Typical scenes include:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Sidewalks\n",
    "* Crosswalks\n",
    "* Shopping districts\n",
    "* Urban pedestrian traffic\n",
    "\n",
    "### Annotation Format\n",
    "\n",
    "The original annotations are stored as MATLAB `.mat` files.\n",
    "\n",
    "For each image:\n",
    "\n",
    "```text\n",
    "IMG_1.jpg\n",
    "IMG_1.mat\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "The `.mat` file contains coordinates:\n",
    "\n",
    "```python\n",
    "[(x1, y1),\n",
    " (x2, y2),\n",
    " ...\n",
    " (xn, yn)]\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "where each coordinate represents the center of a person's head.\n",
    "\n",
    "Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "```python\n",
    "[(120, 45),\n",
    " (230, 89),\n",
    " (450, 150)]\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "meaning the image contains 3 annotated people.\n",
    "\n",
    "The crowd count is simply:\n",
    "\n",
    "> Count Number of annotation points\n",
    "\n",
    "### Density Map Generation\n",
    "\n",
    "Most methods do not directly regress the count. Instead, they generate a **ground-truth density map** by placing a Gaussian kernel at each annotated head location.\n",
    "\n",
    "Conceptually:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "D(x)=\\sum_{i=1}^{N} G(x-x_i)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where:\n",
    "\n",
    "* $x_i$ = head annotation\n",
    "* $G$ = Gaussian kernel\n",
    "* $N$ = number of people\n",
    "\n",
    "The total count is recovered by integrating the density map:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Count = \\sum D(x)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This formulation allows neural networks to learn spatial crowd distributions rather than a single scalar count.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why the Dataset Is Challenging\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Scale Variation\n",
    "\n",
    "A person's head can appear:\n",
    "\n",
    "* 5–10 pixels wide in distant regions\n",
    "* 50+ pixels wide in foreground regions\n",
    "\n",
    "within the same image.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Perspective Distortion\n",
    "\n",
    "People closer to the camera appear much larger than those farther away.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Occlusion\n",
    "\n",
    "In dense crowds:\n",
    "\n",
    "* bodies overlap\n",
    "* faces are hidden\n",
    "* only parts of heads are visible\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Density Variation\n",
    "\n",
    "Crowd density ranges from:\n",
    "\n",
    "* fewer than 10 people\n",
    "* to over 3,000 people\n",
    "\n",
    "in a single image.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Common Evaluation Metrics\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Mean Absolute Error (MAE)\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "MAE=\\frac1N\\sum_{i=1}^{N}|C_i-\\hat C_i|\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Measures average counting error.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "RMSE=\\sqrt{\\frac1N\\sum_{i=1}^{N}(C_i-\\hat C_i)^2}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Penalizes large errors more heavily.\n",
    "\n",
    "Where:\n",
    "\n",
    "* $C_i$ = ground-truth count\n",
    "* $\\hat C_i$ = predicted count\n",
    "\n",
    "These are the standard metrics reported on ShanghaiTech.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Typical Directory Structure\n",
    "\n",
    "```text\n",
    "ShanghaiTech/\n",
    "│\n",
    "├── part_A_final/\n",
    "│   ├── train_data/\n",
    "│   │   ├── images/\n",
    "│   │   └── ground_truth/\n",
    "│   └── test_data/\n",
    "│       ├── images/\n",
    "│       └── ground_truth/\n",
    "│\n",
    "└── part_B_final/\n",
    "    ├── train_data/\n",
    "    │   ├── images/\n",
    "    │   └── ground_truth/\n",
    "    └── test_data/\n",
    "        ├── images/\n",
    "        └── ground_truth/\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "### Research Importance\n",
    "\n",
    "ShanghaiTech became the de facto benchmark for:\n",
    "\n",
    "* MCNN\n",
    "* CSRNet\n",
    "* SANet\n",
    "* CAN\n",
    "* DM-Count\n",
    "* BL\n",
    "* Transformer-based crowd counters\n",
    "* Crowd localization networks\n",
    "\n",
    "Almost every major crowd-counting paper since 2016 reports results on ShanghaiTech A and B, making it the equivalent of \"ImageNet\" for crowd-counting research.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Charecterstics\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Advantages\n",
    "\n",
    "* Large number of annotations (330k+ heads)\n",
    "* Diverse crowd densities\n",
    "* Real-world scenes\n",
    "* Standardized train/test splits\n",
    "* Extensive historical benchmark results available\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Limitations\n",
    "\n",
    "* Only point annotations (no bounding boxes)\n",
    "* Limited geographic diversity\n",
    "* Part B contains mostly urban street scenes\n",
    "* Smaller than newer datasets such as:\n",
    "\n",
    "  * UCF-QNRF\n",
    "  * NWPU-Crowd\n",
    "  * JHU-CROWD++\n",
    "* Does not include temporal/video information.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Quick Summary\n",
    "\n",
    "| Feature         | Part A          | Part B               |\n",
    "| --------------- | --------------- | -------------------- |\n",
    "| Images          | 482             | 716                  |\n",
    "| Train/Test      | 300/182         | 400/316              |\n",
    "| Density         | Very High       | Moderate             |\n",
    "| Source          | Internet        | Shanghai Streets     |\n",
    "| Avg Count/Image | ~501            | ~124                 |\n",
    "| Max Count       | 3139            | 578                  |\n",
    "| Difficulty      | Hard            | Medium               |\n",
    "| Main Challenge  | Extreme Density | Urban Generalization |\n",
    "\n",
    "In practice, **Part A evaluates how well a model handles extremely dense crowds**, while **Part B evaluates performance in more realistic surveillance and street-view scenarios**. Together they provide a balanced benchmark for crowd-counting research. \n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "## UCF_CC_50      \n",
