    "\n",
    "## UCF-QNRF\n",
    "\n",
    "The [UCF-QNRF (UCF-Qatar National Research Fund) Crowd Counting Dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/faihajalamtopu/ucf-qnrf) is one of the most challenging and influential datasets for **crowd counting, density estimation, and crowd localization**. Introduced in 2018 by researchers from the **University of Central Florida (UCF)**, it was designed to overcome the limitations of earlier datasets such as ShanghaiTech and UCF_CC_50 by providing much larger scale, higher resolution, and greater scene diversity. \n",
    "\n",
    "| Property            | Value                       |\n",
    "| ------------------- | --------------------------- |\n",
    "| Release Year        | 2018                        |\n",
    "| Total Images        | 1,535                       |\n",
    "| Training Images     | 1,201                       |\n",
    "| Test Images         | 334                         |\n",
    "| Total Annotations   | 1,251,642 people            |\n",
    "| Annotation Type     | Head-center dot annotations |\n",
    "| Average Count/Image | 815                         |\n",
    "| Maximum Count       | 12,865                      |\n",
    "| Average Resolution  | 2013 × 2902 pixels          |\n",
    "\n",
    "At the time of release, UCF-QNRF contained the **largest number of crowd annotations** among public crowd-counting datasets, with over **1.25 million manually annotated heads**. \n",
    "\n",
    "### Motivation Behind the Dataset\n",
    "\n",
    "Earlier datasets had several shortcomings:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Dataset      | Limitation                                    |\n",
    "| ------------ | --------------------------------------------- |\n",
    "| UCF_CC_50    | Only 50 images                                |\n",
    "| ShanghaiTech | Limited scene diversity and annotation volume |\n",
    "| WorldExpo'10 | Mostly surveillance-camera viewpoints         |\n",
    "\n",
    "The creators of UCF-QNRF wanted a dataset that:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Contains extremely dense crowds.\n",
    "* Covers multiple countries and event types.\n",
    "* Includes significant perspective distortion.\n",
    "* Provides high-resolution images suitable for localization.\n",
    "* Supports counting, density estimation, and localization simultaneously.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Data Collection\n",
    "\n",
    "Images were collected from:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Web searches\n",
    "* Flickr photographs\n",
    "* News images\n",
    "* Public event photographs\n",
    "* Pilgrimages\n",
    "* Festivals\n",
    "* Demonstrations\n",
    "* Sports events\n",
    "* Urban gatherings\n",
    "\n",
    "Unlike ShanghaiTech Part A, many UCF-QNRF images preserve the **full scene context**, including:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Buildings\n",
    "* Roads\n",
    "* Vegetation\n",
    "* Sky\n",
    "* Vehicles\n",
    "\n",
    "This makes the dataset more realistic and significantly harder.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Crowd Density Characteristics\n",
    "\n",
    "One of the most important aspects of UCF-QNRF is its enormous variation in crowd density.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Statistic     | Value         |\n",
    "| ------------- | ------------- |\n",
    "| Minimum Count | ~49–65 people |\n",
    "| Average Count | ~815 people   |\n",
    "| Maximum Count | 12,865 people |\n",
    "\n",
    "The largest image contains nearly **13,000 annotated individuals**, which is more than four times the maximum count in ShanghaiTech Part A.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Resolution and Scale Variation\n",
    "\n",
    "The dataset contains extremely high-resolution images.\n",
    "\n",
    "Average resolution:\n",
    "\n",
    "```text\n",
    "2013 × 2902 pixels\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "Some images exceed:\n",
    "\n",
    "```text\n",
    "9000 × 6000 pixels\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "This creates severe scale variation:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Nearby heads may occupy hundreds of pixels.\n",
    "* Distant heads may occupy only a few pixels.\n",
    "* Head sizes can vary dramatically within a single image.\n",
    "\n",
    "These characteristics make scale-aware architectures essential.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Annotation Format\n",
    "\n",
    "Each person is annotated using a **single dot at the center of the head**.\n",
    "\n",
    "Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "```python\n",
    "[\n",
    " (120, 65),\n",
    " (132, 68),\n",
    " (450, 200),\n",
    " ...\n",
    "]\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "The total crowd count is simply:\n",
    "\n",
    "> Count = Number of annotated points\n",
    "\n",
    "Annotations are provided for every visible person in the image. The dataset was specifically designed to support:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Crowd counting\n",
    "2. Density map estimation\n",
    "3. Crowd localization\n",
    "\n",
    "simultaneously.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Density Map Generation\n",
    "\n",
    "Most deep-learning methods transform point annotations into density maps.\n",
    "\n",
    "The ground-truth density function is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "D(x)=\\sum_{i=1}^{N}G(x-x_i)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where:\n",
    "\n",
    "* $x_i$ = annotated head location\n",
    "* $G$ = Gaussian kernel\n",
    "* $N$ = number of people\n",
    "\n",
    "The crowd count is recovered by integrating the density map:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Count=\\sum D(x)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Density-map learning remains the dominant training strategy on UCF-QNRF.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Major Challenges\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Extreme Density Variation\n",
    "\n",
    "Images can range from:\n",
    "\n",
    "* sparse crowds (~50 people)\n",
    "* ultra-dense crowds (>12,000 people)\n",
    "\n",
    "within the same dataset. \n",
    "\n",
    "#### Scale Variation\n",
    "\n",
    "A head can appear:\n",
    "\n",
    "* extremely small in distant regions\n",
    "* very large in foreground regions\n",
    "\n",
    "sometimes within the same image.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Perspective Distortion\n",
    "\n",
    "Images are captured from:\n",
    "\n",
    "* aerial views\n",
    "* elevated viewpoints\n",
    "* ground-level photography\n",
    "* oblique camera angles\n",
    "\n",
    "making perspective handling a major challenge.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Background Complexity\n",
    "\n",
    "Unlike heavily cropped crowd datasets, UCF-QNRF includes:\n",
    "\n",
    "* buildings\n",
    "* roads\n",
    "* trees\n",
    "* mountains\n",
    "* sky\n",
    "\n",
    "Models must learn to ignore large non-crowd regions. \n",
    "\n",
    "#### Illumination Variation\n",
    "\n",
    "The dataset contains:\n",
    "\n",
    "* daylight scenes\n",
    "* nighttime scenes\n",
    "* indoor gatherings\n",
    "* outdoor events\n",
    "* shadows\n",
    "* weather effects\n",
    "\n",
    "This diversity improves generalization but increases difficulty.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Train-Test Split\n",
    "\n",
    "The official split is:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Set      | Images |\n",
    "| -------- | ------ |\n",
    "| Training | 1,201  |\n",
    "| Testing  | 334    |\n",
    "| Total    | 1,535  |\n",
    "\n",
    "The split was created by sorting images by crowd count and selecting every fifth image for testing, ensuring similar count distributions across train and test sets.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Evaluation Metrics\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Mean Absolute Error (MAE)\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "MAE=\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}|C_i-\\hat{C}_i|\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Measures average counting error.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "RMSE=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}(C_i-\\hat{C}_i)^2}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Penalizes large mistakes more heavily.\n",
    "\n",
    "Where:\n",
    "\n",
    "* $C_i$ = ground truth count\n",
    "* $\\hat{C}_i$ = predicted count\n",
    "\n",
    "These are the standard benchmarks reported on UCF-QNRF.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Typical Directory Structure\n",
    "\n",
    "```text\n",
    "UCF-QNRF/\n",
    "│\n",
    "├── Train/\n",
    "│   ├── Images/\n",
    "│   └── Ground_Truth/\n",
    "│\n",
    "├── Test/\n",
    "│   ├── Images/\n",
    "│   └── Ground_Truth/\n",
    "│\n",
    "└── Readme.txt\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "Ground-truth files typically contain head coordinates for each image.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Comparison with ShanghaiTech\n",
    "\n",
    "| Feature         | ShanghaiTech A | UCF-QNRF  |\n",
    "| --------------- | -------------- | --------- |\n",
    "| Images          | 482            | 1,535     |\n",
    "| Annotations     | 241,677        | 1,251,642 |\n",
    "| Avg Count       | 501            | 815       |\n",
    "| Max Count       | 3,139          | 12,865    |\n",
    "| Avg Resolution  | 589×868        | 2013×2902 |\n",
    "| Scene Diversity | Moderate       | Very High |\n",
    "| Difficulty      | High           | Very High |\n",
    "\n",
    "UCF-QNRF is generally regarded as significantly harder than ShanghaiTech because it combines:\n",
    "\n",
    "* higher image resolution,\n",
    "* larger crowd counts,\n",
    "* broader geographic diversity,\n",
    "* stronger scale variation,\n",
    "* more complex backgrounds.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Research Importance\n",
    "\n",
    "UCF-QNRF has become one of the primary benchmarks for modern crowd-counting models, including:\n",
    "\n",
    "* CSRNet\n",
    "* CAN\n",
    "* SANet\n",
    "* DM-Count\n",
    "* BL\n",
    "* Transformer-based crowd counters\n",
    "* Localization-based crowd analysis systems\n",
    "\n",
    "Because of its size and diversity, strong performance on UCF-QNRF is often considered a good indicator of a model's real-world robustness.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Quick Summary\n",
    "\n",
    "**UCF-QNRF** is a large-scale, high-resolution crowd-counting dataset containing **1,535 images and over 1.25 million annotated people**. Its defining features are **extreme crowd densities (up to 12,865 people in a single image), massive scale variation, diverse real-world scenes, and complex backgrounds**, making it one of the most challenging and widely used benchmarks for crowd counting, density estimation, and crowd localization research. \n",
    "\n",
